# Working on Ksav and sefer-crates at the same time.
#
# Copy this file to `.cargo/config.toml` (beside it, git-ignored) and every
# `girsa-*` crate resolves to a local checkout instead of the pinned commit.
# Cargo discovers config by walking up from the directory it was invoked in, so
# one copy at the repository root covers `ksav/engine`, `ksav/wasm` and
# `ksav/app/src-tauri` alike. Delete it and the pin is back; nothing else in the
# repository knows the difference.
#
# # Why this exists
#
# The shared crates used to be `path = "../../../sefer-crates/crates/…"`, a
# sibling of the checkout root. That bought exactly one thing and it is a real
# thing: Girsa and Ksav are two halves of one product, and a change to what a
# quote block *is* has to be visible on both sides as one edit, not as a
# release. What it cost was that `git clone ksav && cargo build` failed at
# `cargo metadata` — before a compiler ran, naming a directory the reader had
# never heard of, with no documentation anywhere saying why.
#
# Those are separable, and this file is the separation. The manifests pin a
# commit, so a clone builds. This file, when you make it, restores the sibling
# checkout for the days you are moving the seam.
#
# # Path overrides, not `[patch]`
#
# Both work; they were tried side by side, and the difference is what they do to
# `Cargo.lock`. `[patch]` re-resolves, so cargo rewrites the lock and **the git
# pin disappears from it** — measured, not assumed: five `sefer-crates` entries
# to zero on the first `cargo metadata`. A week of paired work would leave that
# dirty in every diff, and one distracted `git add -A` would commit a lock file
# with no pin in it, which is the fresh-clone build broken again by the fix for
# it. (`engine/tests/manifests.rs` would catch that, and being caught by a test
# is worse than not doing it.) A `paths` override leaves the lock byte-identical,
# because it substitutes sources for crates already resolved and cannot change a
# version or a dependency list.
#
# The price is a warning on every invocation:
#
#   warning: path override for crate `girsa-ksav` has altered the original list
#   of dependencies; the dependency on `girsa-source` was either added or
#   modified to not match the previously resolved version
#
# That is expected and harmless *here*: `girsa-ksav` reaches `girsa-source`
# through the workspace instead of through git, and every crate in the chain is
# overridden together, so the graph stays consistent. Cargo cannot see that from
# one crate at a time. If you ever override some and not others, believe the
# warning.
#
# # The layout this assumes
#
#   Videos/
#     Ksav/            <- this repository (the path below is relative to here)
#     sefer-crates/    <- git clone https://github.com/SYKhayyat/sefer-crates
#     Girsa/           <- the other half, which reaches the same checkout
#
# Adjust the paths if yours differs. They are relative to the *parent* of the
# directory holding this file — that is, to the repository root, not to
# `.cargo/`. Cargo says so if you get it wrong, and says it loudly: a path
# override that does not resolve is a hard error naming the config file, not a
# silent fall-through to the pin. Which is the right failure — an override that
# quietly stopped applying would be a day of wondering why an edit next door
# changed nothing.

paths = [
    "../sefer-crates/crates/girsa-source",
    "../sefer-crates/crates/girsa-ksav",
    "../sefer-crates/crates/girsa-post",
    "../sefer-crates/crates/girsa-ref",
    "../sefer-crates/crates/girsa-hebrew",
]
